# Derivation provenance vocabulary — LOAD THIS into a working repository.
#
# The terms an application writes onto a relationship's quoted triple to record that the relationship
# came from a derivation rather than from someone drawing it. Nothing about the ArchiMate language
# requires them: a deployment that does no derivation has no use for them, and an exchange file has
# nowhere to put them. That is why they are here rather than in the base ontology.
#
# WHERE TO LOAD THEM: the default graph, or wherever ordinary SPARQL can see them. These are read back
# by applications — to show a modeller which rule produced a relationship, and whether it was a valid
# derivation or a suggested one. Some engines hide the SHACL shapes graph from ordinary queries (RDF4J
# does), so a deployment that puts archimate_derivation_rules.ttl in a shapes graph must still load
# this file outside it.
#
# NOT derivation, despite the similar name: archimate:influenceStrength stays in
# ontology/archimate.ttl. That is the specification's own attribute on an influence relationship
# (weak / moderate / strong), unrelated to the weakest-link ordering in
# archimate_derivation_strengths.ttl.

@base <https://purl.org/archimate#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix sh:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<#DerivationCertainty> rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Derivation certainty" ;
    rdfs:comment "How far a derived relationship is to be trusted. Appendix B draws the line: a valid derivation is one the specification permits a modeller to assert, a potential derivation is one it merely suggests. Two individuals exhaust it." .

<#ValidDerivation> rdf:type <#DerivationCertainty> ;
    rdfs:label "valid derivation" ;
    rdfs:comment "Produced by one of Appendix B's valid derivation rules (DR1-DR8). The specification permits a modeller to assert the result." .

<#PotentialDerivation> rdf:type <#DerivationCertainty> ;
    rdfs:label "potential derivation" ;
    rdfs:comment "Produced by one of Appendix B's potential derivation rules (PDR1-PDR12). B.3 says such a relationship \"might be relevant but may also be wrong… it is up to the modeler to decide\", so asserting it without a modeller's judgement asserts something the specification does not sanction." .

<#confidence> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "Confidence" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a derived relationship's quoted triple: how far the derivation is to be trusted. Points at <#ValidDerivation> or <#PotentialDerivation> — an IRI rather than a string, so the distinction can be joined against and carries its own definition." ;
    rdfs:range <#DerivationCertainty> .

<#derivedFrom> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "derived from" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a derived relationship's quoted triple: the quoted source triples the derivation consumed — the actual relationships, not their types, so a derived relationship can be traced back to the specific edges that justified it. Every rule writes one per premise it matched." .

<#derivationRule> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "derivation rule" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a derived relationship's quoted triple: the rule that produced it, as the rule's own IRI (e.g. deriv:DR2_StructuralChain). An IRI rather than a name, so a query reaches the rule's label, comment and Appendix B reference without matching substrings — \"PDR1\" is a prefix of PDR10, PDR11 and PDR12." ;
    rdfs:range sh:SPARQLRule .

<#description> rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "description" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a derived relationship's quoted triple: a human-readable note on how it arose, e.g. \"Derived via DR2: structural relationship chain\". Written by every rule in archimate_derivation_rules.ttl." ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

<#createdBy> rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "created by" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a derived relationship's quoted triple: what produced it — an engine name, or a user identifier where a modeller accepted a suggestion." ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

<#createdDate> rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "created date" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a derived relationship's quoted triple: when it was produced." ;
    rdfs:range xsd:dateTime .
